{
 "id": 117,
 "number": 329,
 "title": "Longest Increasing Path in a Matrix",
 "slug": "longest-increasing-path-in-a-matrix",
 "category": "2d-dp",
 "difficulty": "Hard",
 "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-increasing-path-in-a-matrix/",
 "pattern": "DFS with memoisation",
 "statement": "Given a matrix of integers, return the length of the longest path in which every step moves up, down, left or right to a cell with a **strictly larger** value.",
 "example": {
  "input": "matrix = [[9,9,4],[6,6,8],[2,1,1]]",
  "output": "4",
  "why": "1 → 2 → 6 → 9 (bottom middle, bottom left, middle left, top left)."
 },
 "more": [
  {
   "input": "matrix = [[3,4,5],[3,2,6],[2,2,1]]",
   "output": "4"
  },
  {
   "input": "matrix = [[1]]",
   "output": "1"
  },
  {
   "input": "matrix = [[7,7],[7,7]]",
   "output": "1"
  },
  {
   "input": "matrix = [[1,2,3,4,5]]",
   "output": "5"
  }
 ],
 "idea": [
  "For a single cell, the longest increasing path **starting there** is 1 plus the best path starting at any larger neighbour.",
  "Paths only climb to larger values, so they can never loop — the recursion always ends.",
  "Many paths pass through the same cells, so **remember** each cell's answer (`dp`). Every cell is then solved exactly once, and the overall answer is the largest value in `dp`."
 ],
 "code": "class Solution:\n    def longestIncreasingPath(self, matrix: List[List[int]]) -> int:\n        if not matrix:\n            return 0\n\n        # Define directions for moving to neighboring cells: up, down, left, right.\n        directions = [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]\n\n        # Function to perform DFS from a given cell (i, j).\n        def dfs(i, j):\n            # If the result for this cell is already calculated, return it.\n            if dp[i][j] != -1:\n                return dp[i][j]\n\n            # Initialize the result for this cell to 1 (counting itself).\n            dp[i][j] = 1\n\n            # Explore the four neighboring cells.\n            for dx, dy in directions:\n                x, y = i + dx, j + dy\n                if 0 <= x < m and 0 <= y < n and matrix[x][y] > matrix[i][j]:\n                    # If the neighboring cell has a larger value, perform DFS.\n                    dp[i][j] = max(dp[i][j], 1 + dfs(x, y))\n\n            return dp[i][j]\n\n        m, n = len(matrix), len(matrix[0])\n        dp = [[-1] * n for _ in range(m)]  # Memoization table to store results.\n        max_path = 0\n\n        # Start DFS from each cell in the matrix.\n        for i in range(m):\n            for j in range(n):\n                max_path = max(max_path, dfs(i, j))\n\n        return max_path",
 "complexity": {
  "time": "O(m · n)",
  "timeWhy": "Thanks to the memo table every cell is solved once, looking at four neighbours.",
  "space": "O(m · n)",
  "spaceWhy": "The memo table and, at worst, a recursion as deep as the number of cells."
 },
